rt`  : Reverts a previous commit \\n\\nFor example, a PR title could be: \\n\\n\\\\-  `refactor: modify package path` \\n\\n\\\\-  `feat(frontend): xxxx`  , where  `(frontend)`  means that this PR mainly focuses on the frontend  component. \\n\\nYou may also check out previous PRs in the  [  PR list  ](  https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls  )  . \\n\\n### 2. Pull Request description \\n\\n\\\\-  If your PR is small (such as a typo fix), you can  go brief. \\n\\n\\\\-  If it contains a lot of changes, it's better to wr  ite more details. \\n\\nEnter to Rename, Shift+Enter to Preview \\n\\nLoading \\n\\nCommit message \\n\\nUpdate contribution.md\\n\\nTarget Branch \\n\\nStart a **new merge request** with these changes \\n\\nCommit changes  Loading  Commit changes  [ Cancel  ](/root/sotopia/-/blob/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md)\\n\", url='http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia/-/edit/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads', trigger_by_action='browse_interactive', screenshot_path='/outputs/.browser_screenshots/screenshot_20251101_110058_375479.png', error=False, observation=<ObservationType.BROWSE: 'browse'>, goal_image_urls=[], open_pages_urls=['http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia/-/edit/main/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md?ref_type=heads'], active_page_index=0, last_browser_action=\"\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\npress('1281', 'ControlOrMeta+End')  # Navigate to the end of the file\\nnoop(500)\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\", last_browser_action_error='', focused_element_bid='1281'), BrowseInteractiveAction(browser_actions='\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nfill(\\'1281\\', \\'- `feat(frontend): xxxx`, where `(frontend)` means that this PR mainly focuses on the frontend component.\\\\n\\\\nYou may also check out previous PRs in the [PR list](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls).\\\\n\\\\n### 2. Pull Request description\\\\n- If your PR is small (such as a typo fix), you can go brief.\\\\n- If it contains a lot of changes, it\\\\\\'s better to write more details.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Environments\\\\n\\\\n### Using VS Code with Dev Containers\\\\n\\\\nVS Code Dev Containers provide a consistent development environment that works across different machines. To use Dev Containers with VS Code:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Install the Dev Containers extension**:\\\\n   - Open VS Code\\\\n   - Go to Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X)\\\\n   - Search for \\\\\"Dev Containers\\\\\" and install it\\\\n\\\\n2. **Open the project in a container**:\\\\n   - Open the Sotopia repository in VS Code\\\\n   - When prompted, click \\\\\"Reopen in Container\\\\\"\\\\n   - If not prompted, press Ctrl+Shift+P and run \\\\\"Dev Containers: Reopen in Container\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n3. **Development workflow**:\\\\n   - The container will automatically build with all dependencies installed\\\\n   - Your code will be synchronized between the container and your local machine\\\\n   - Use the integrated terminal in VS Code to run commands\\\\n\\\\n### Using Other IDEs or Editors with Dev Containers\\\\n\\\\nIf you prefer other IDEs or editors, you can still use Dev Containers:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Docker Compose approach**:\\\\n   ```bash\\\\n   docker-compose up -d\\\\n   docker-compose exec app bash\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n2. **JetBrains IDEs (IntelliJ, PyCharm, etc.)**:\\\\n   - Install the Docker plugin\\\\n   - Configure a Docker interpreter for your project\\\\n   - Run and debug your code within the container\\\\n\\\\n3. **Command line development**:\\\\n   ```bash\\\\n   # Build and run the container\\\\n   docker build -t sotopia-dev .\\\\n   docker run -it --rm -v $(pwd):/workspace sotopia-dev bash\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n### Without Dev Containers\\\\n\\\\nIf you prefer to develop without containers, you can set up the environment manually:\\\\n\\\\n1. **System requirements**:\\\\n   - Python 3.8+ \\\\n   - Poetry for dependency management\\\\n   - Redis server\\\\n   - Node.js (for documentation)\\\\n\\\\n2. **Installation steps**:\\\\n   ```bash\\\\n   # Clone the repository\\\\n   git clone https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia.git\\\\n   cd sotopia\\\\n\\\\n   # Install Python dependencies\\\\n   poetry install\\\\n\\\\n   # Install documentation dependencies\\\\n   cd docs\\\\n   npm install\\\\n   cd ..\\\\n\\\\n   # Start Redis (if not already running)\\\\n   redis-server\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n3. **Development workflow**:\\\\n   - Activate the virtual environment: `poetry shell`\\\\n   - Run tests: `pytest`\\\\n   - Start development server: `python -m sotopia`\\\\n   - Build documentation: `cd docs && npm run build`\\')\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n', thought=\"Perfect! Now I'm at the end of the file. I can see the current content ends with the PR description section. Now I need to add the three required sections about development environments. Let me add them at the end of the file.\", browsergym_send_msg_to_user='', action=<ActionType.BROWSE_INTERACTIVE: 'browse_interactive'>, security_risk=None), BrowserOutputObservation(content='Browser environment took too long to respond.', url='', trigger_by_action='browse_interactive', screenshot_path=None, error=True, observation=<ObservationType.BROWSE: 'browse'>, goal_image_urls=[], open_pages_urls=[], active_page_index=-1, last_browser_action='', last_browser_action_error='Browser environment took too long to respond.', focused_element_bid=''), BrowseInteractiveAction(browser_actions=\"\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\npress('1281', 'Enter')  # Add a new line at the end\\nnoop(500)\\npress('1281', 'Enter')  # Add another new line\\nnoop(500)\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\", thought='The browser seems to have timed out. Let me try a simpler approach and add the content in smaller chunks.', browsergym_send_msg_to_user='', action=<ActionType.BROWSE_INTERACTIVE: 'browse_interactive'>, security_risk=None), BrowserOutputObservation(content='Browser environment took too long to respond.', url='', trigger_by_action='browse_interactive', screenshot_path=None, error=True, observation=<ObservationType.BROWSE: 'browse'>, goal_image_urls=[], open_pages_urls=[], active_page_index=-1, last_browser_action='', last_browser_action_error='Browser environment took too long to respond.', focused_element_bid=''), CmdRunAction(command='\\ncd /workspace && git clone http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia.git\\n', is_input=False, thought=\"It seems the browser is having issues. Let me try a different approach - I'll first clone the repository locally, make the changes, and then push them to create a merge request.\", blocking=False, is_static=False, cwd=None, hidden=False, action=<ActionType.RUN: 'run'>, confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content=\"Cloning into 'sotopia'...\\nremote: Enumerating objects: 2580, done.\\nremote: Counting objects: 100% (576/576), done.\\nremote: Compressing objects: 100% (288/288), done.\\nremote: Total 2580 (delta 461), reused 288 (delta 288), pack-reused 2004 (from 1)\\nReceiving objects: 100% (2580/2580), 4.08 MiB | 40.93 MiB/s, done.\\nResolving deltas: 100% (1457/1457), done.\", command='cd /workspace && git clone http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/sotopia.git', observation=<ObservationType.RUN: 'run'>, metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ecs-d984', working_dir='/workspace', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), CmdRunAction(command='\\n\\ncd /workspace/sotopia && ls -la docs/pages/contribution/\\n\\n', is_input=False, thought='Great! The repository has been cloned successfully. Now let me navigate to the contribution document and view its current content.', blocking=False, is_static=False, cwd=None, hidden=False, action=<ActionType.RUN: 'run'>, confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content='total 16\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Nov  1 11:05 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 6 root root 4096 Nov  1 11:05 ..\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 4395 Nov  1 11:05 contribution.md', command='cd /workspace/sotopia && ls -la docs/pages/contribution/', observation=<ObservationType.RUN: 'run'>, metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ecs-d984', working_dir='/workspace/sotopia', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), FileReadAction(path='/workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md', start=0, end=-1, thought='Perfect! Now let me view the current content of the contribution.md file to understand its structure.', action=<ActionType.READ: 'read'>, security_risk=None, impl_source='oh_aci', view_range=None), FileReadObservation(content=\"Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md:\\n     1\\t# Contributing\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tThanks for your interest in contributing to Sotopia! We welcome and appreciate contributions.\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t## How Can I Contribute?\\n     6\\t\\n     7\\tThere are many ways that you can contribute:\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t1. **Download and use** Sotopia, and send [issues](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/issues) when you encounter something that isn't working or a feature that you'd like to see.\\n    10\\t2. **Send feedback** after each session by using our feedback mechanisms (if implemented), so we can see where things are working and failing, and also build an open dataset for training social agents.\\n    11\\t3. **Improve the Codebase** by sending PRs (see details below). In particular, we have some [good first issue](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/labels/good%20first%20issue) issues that may be ones to start on.\\n    12\\t\\n    13\\t## Understanding Sotopia's CodeBase\\n    14\\t\\n    15\\tTo understand the codebase, please refer to the README in each module:\\n    16\\t- [sotopia](./sotopia/README.md)\\n    17\\t- [docs](./docs/README.md)\\n    18\\t- [tests](./tests/README.md)\\n    19\\t\\n    20\\tWhen you write code, it is also good to write tests. Please navigate to the `tests` folder to see existing test suites.\\n    21\\tAt the moment, we have different kinds of tests including unit tests and integration tests. Please refer to the README for each test suite. These tests also run on GitHub's continuous integration to ensure quality of the project.\\n    22\\t\\n    23\\t## Sending Pull Requests to Sotopia\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t### 1. Fork the Official Repository\\n    26\\tFork the [Sotopia repository](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia) into your own account.\\n    27\\tClone your own forked repository into your local environment:\\n    28\\t\\n    29\\t```shell\\n    30\\tgit clone git@github.com:<YOUR-USERNAME>/sotopia.git\\n    31\\t```\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t### 2. Configure Git\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\tSet the official repository as your [upstream](https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials/git-forks-and-upstreams) to synchronize with the latest update in the official repository.\\n    36\\tAdd the original repository as upstream:\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t```shell\\n    39\\tcd sotopia\\n    40\\tgit remote add upstream git@github.com:sotopia-lab/sotopia.git\\n    41\\t```\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\tVerify that the remote is set:\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\t```shell\\n    46\\tgit remote -v\\n    47\\t```\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\tYou should see both `origin` and `upstream` in the output.\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t### 3. Synchronize with Official Repository\\n    52\\tSynchronize latest commit with official repository before coding:\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t```shell\\n    55\\tgit fetch upstream\\n    56\\tgit checkout main\\n    57\\tgit merge upstream/main\\n    58\\tgit push origin main\\n    59\\t```\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t### 4. Set up the Development Environment\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\t?? @ProKil\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\t### 5. Write Code and Commit It\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\tOnce you have done this, you can write code, test it, and commit it to a branch (replace `my_branch` with an appropriate name):\\n    68\\t\\n    69\\t```shell\\n    70\\tgit checkout -b my_branch\\n    71\\tgit add .\\n    72\\tgit commit\\n    73\\tgit push origin my_branch\\n    74\\t```\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\t### 6. Open a Pull Request\\n    77\\t\\n    78\\t* On GitHub, go to the page of your forked repository, and create a Pull Request:\\n    79\\t   - Click on `Branches`\\n    80\\t   - Click on the `...` beside your branch and click on `New pull request`\\n    81\\t   - Set `base repository` to `sotopia-lab/sotopia`\\n    82\\t   - Set `base` to `main`\\n    83\\t   - Click `Create pull request`\\n    84\\t\\n    85\\tThe PR should appear in [Sotopia PRs](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls).\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\tThen the Sotopia team will review your code.\\n    88\\t\\n    89\\t## PR Rules\\n    90\\t\\n    91\\t### 1. Pull Request title\\n    92\\tAs described [here](https://github.com/commitizen/conventional-commit-types/blob/master/index.json), a valid PR title should begin with one of the following prefixes:\\n    93\\t\\n    94\\t- `feat`: A new feature\\n    95\\t- `fix`: A bug fix\\n    96\\t- `docs`: Documentation only changes\\n    97\\t- `style`: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code (white space, formatting, missing semicolons, etc.)\\n    98\\t- `refactor`: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature\\n    99\\t- `perf`: A code change that improves performance\\n   100\\t- `test`: Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests\\n   101\\t- `build`: Changes that affect the build system or external dependencies (example scopes: gulp, broccoli, npm)\\n   102\\t- `ci`: Changes to our CI configuration files and scripts (example scopes: Travis, Circle, BrowserStack, SauceLabs)\\n   103\\t- `chore`: Other changes that don't modify src or test files\\n   104\\t- `revert`: Reverts a previous commit\\n   105\\t\\n   106\\tFor example, a PR title could be:\\n   107\\t- `refactor: modify package path`\\n   108\\t- `feat(frontend): xxxx`, where `(frontend)` means that this PR mainly focuses on the frontend component.\\n   109\\t\\n   110\\tYou may also check out previous PRs in the [PR list](https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia/pulls).\\n   111\\t\\n   112\\t### 2. Pull Request description\\n   113\\t- If your PR is small (such as a typo fix), you can go brief.\\n   114\\t- If it contains a lot of changes, it's better to write more details.\\n   115\\t\\n\", path='/workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md', observation=<ObservationType.READ: 'read'>, impl_source='oh_aci'), **FileEditAction**\nPath: [/workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md]\nThought: Perfect! I can see the current content of the contribution.md file. I notice there's a placeholder \"?? @ProKil\" in section 4 about setting up the development environment. This is the perfect place to add the three required sections about development environments. Let me update the file with the new content.\nCommand: str_replace\nOld String: ```\n### 4. Set up the Development Environment\n\n?? @ProKil\n```\nNew String: ```\n### 4. Set up the Development Environment\n\nYou can set up your development environment using different approaches:\n\n#### Using VS Code with Dev Containers\n\nVS Code Dev Containers provide a consistent development environment that works across different machines. To use Dev Containers with VS Code:\n\n1. **Install the Dev Containers extension**:\n   - Open VS Code\n   - Go to Extensions (Ctrl+Shift+X)\n   - Search for \"Dev Containers\" and install it\n\n2. **Open the project in a container**:\n   - Open the Sotopia repository in VS Code\n   - When prompted, click \"Reopen in Container\"\n   - If not prompted, press Ctrl+Shift+P and run \"Dev Containers: Reopen in Container\"\n\n3. **Development workflow**:\n   - The container will automatically build with all dependencies installed\n   - Your code will be synchronized between the container and your local machine\n   - Use the integrated terminal in VS Code to run commands\n\n#### Using Other IDEs or Editors with Dev Containers\n\nIf you prefer other IDEs or editors, you can still use Dev Containers:\n\n1. **Docker Compose approach**:\n   ```bash\n   docker-compose up -d\n   docker-compose exec app bash\n   ```\n\n2. **JetBrains IDEs (IntelliJ, PyCharm, etc.)**:\n   - Install the Docker plugin\n   - Configure a Docker interpreter for your project\n   - Run and debug your code within the container\n\n3. **Command line development**:\n   ```bash\n   # Build and run the container\n   docker build -t sotopia-dev .\n   docker run -it --rm -v $(pwd):/workspace sotopia-dev bash\n   ```\n\n#### Without Dev Containers\n\nIf you prefer to develop without containers, you can set up the environment manually:\n\n1. **System requirements**:\n   - Python 3.8+ \n   - Poetry for dependency management\n   - Redis server\n   - Node.js (for documentation)\n\n2. **Installation steps**:\n   ```bash\n   # Clone the repository\n   git clone https://github.com/sotopia-lab/sotopia.git\n   cd sotopia\n\n   # Install Python dependencies\n   poetry install\n\n   # Install documentation dependencies\n   cd docs\n   npm install\n   cd ..\n\n   # Start Redis (if not already running)\n   redis-server\n   ```\n\n3. **Development workflow**:\n   - Activate the virtual environment: `poetry shell`\n   - Run tests: `pytest`\n   - Start development server: `python -m sotopia`\n   - Build documentation: `cd docs && npm run build`\n```\n, FileEditObservation(content='The file /workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md has been edited. Here\\'s the result of running `cat -n` on a snippet of /workspace/sotopia/docs/pages/contribution/contribution.md:\\n    58\\tgit push origin main\\n    59\\t```\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t### 4. Set up the Development Environment\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\tYou ca